{\it Item-included test-positivity probability:} For any item $i$ in a test, the quantity ${P(-,{q})}$ denotes the probability that  the test has a positive outcome conditioned on the event that item $i$ is non-defective.\footnote{ Due to the symmetry of randomness in the defective set ${{\cal D}}$ this value is independent of the index value $i$, hence in our notation we do not index the notation for these probabilities with $i$.} Analogously ${P(+,{q})}$ denotes$^{\ref{fn:no-i}}$ the probability that the test has a positive outcome conditioned on the event that item $i$ is defective. Mathematically,
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
{P(-,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d} \binom{d}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-j} f(j),\text{ and} \\
{P(+,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{d-1}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-1-j} f(j+1).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{aligned}
{P(-,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d} \binom{d}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-j} f(j),\text{ and} \\
{P(+,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{d-1}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-1-j} f(j+1).
\end{aligned}

{\it Item-not-included test-positivity probability:} For any item $i$ not in a test, the quantity ${Q(-,{q})}$ denotes$^{\ref{fn:no-i}}$ the probability (over the randomness in the defective set ${{\cal D}}$) that the test has a positive outcome conditioned on the event that item $i$ is non-defective. Analogously, ${Q(+,{q})}$, denotes$^{\ref{fn:no-i}}$ the probability that the test has a positive outcome conditioned on the event that item $i$ is defective. Mathematically,
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
{Q(-,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d} \binom{d}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-j} f(j),\text{ and} \\
{Q(+,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{d-1}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-1-j} f(j).
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{aligned}
{Q(-,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d} \binom{d}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-j} f(j),\text{ and} \\
{Q(+,{q})} &:= \sum\limits_{j=0}^{d-1} \binom{d-1}{j} {q}^j (1-{q})^{d-1-j} f(j).
\end{aligned}

{\it Item test sensitivity:} For any item $i$ in a test, its test sensitivity ${\Delta({q})}$ (respectively ${\nabla({q})}$) is defined as the difference between the probabilities of positive test outcomes conditioned on item $i$ being defective or not.$^{\ref{fn:no-i}}$ Mathematically,
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
    &{\Delta({q})} := {P(+,{q})} - {P(-,{q})}, \text{ and} \\
    &{\nabla({q})} := {Q(-,{q})} - {Q(+,{q})} .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{aligned}
    &{\Delta({q})} := {P(+,{q})} - {P(-,{q})}, \text{ and} \\
    &{\nabla({q})} := {Q(-,{q})} - {Q(+,{q})} .
\end{aligned}

{\it Minimal test sensitivity:} A parameter that will be useful in our code design and analysis is the minimal test sensitivity ${P_{\min}({q})}$, defined as
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
    &{P_{\min}({q})} := \min\left\{ {P(+,{q})},1-{Q(+,{q})} \right\}.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}

\begin{aligned}
    &{P_{\min}({q})} := \min\left\{ {P(+,{q})},1-{Q(+,{q})} \right\}.
\end{aligned}

For all ${q}\in(0,1)$, we have
\begin{align} f(d)\ge  {P(+,{q})} > {P(-,{q})} = {Q(-,{q})} > {Q(+,{q})} \ge f(0),
\end{align}
\begin{align} {\nabla({q})} = \frac{{q}}{1-{q}} {\Delta({q})},
\end{align}
\begin{align} 1\geq {P_{\min}({q})} \geq \max \left\{{\Delta({q})}, {\nabla({q})}\right\}.
\end{align}

{\it Test participation parameter:} It also helps to define the test participation parameter ${m}$ as in~\eqref{eq:def:m} below.

\begin{equation} {m} := \frac{8.32{P_{\min}({q})}}{\left({\Delta({q})}\right)^2} \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon } \right) .
\end{equation}

Correspondingly, we define the test participation parameter ${s}$ as below.
\begin{equation} {s} := \frac{8.32{P_{\min}({q})}}{\left({\nabla({q})}\right)^2} \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon } \right) .
\end{equation}

Finally, we set the number of tests as any integer $T$ such that
\begin{equation} \begin{aligned}
 T  \geq {{\Gamma}({q})}:=  \frac{13(1-{q})}{3{q}} {m} ,
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
e.g., we can set $T = \lceil {{\Gamma}({q})} \rceil$.

