Instead, we consider the quantity ${\hat{\Gamma}} ({q})$ defined as
\begin{equation} {\hat{\Gamma}} ({q}) := \frac{{{\Gamma}({q})}}{{P_{\min}({q})}}  = \frac{36.06(1-{q})}{{q} ({\Delta({q})})^2} \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon} \right).
\end{equation}

We will choose ${q}$ as
\begin{equation} {{q}^*} := \mathrm{argmin}_{{q}\in(0,1)} \frac{36.06(1-{q})}{{q} ({\Delta({q})})^2} \log \left( \frac{2n}{\varepsilon} \right).
\end{equation}

{\underline{\it Decoding Rule 1}:} The first decoding rule makes use of the tests that each item participates in. More precisely, denote by ${{m}_{i}}$ the number of tests that item $i$ participates in, and denote by ${{m}_{i}^+}$ (respectively ${{m}_{i}^-}$) the number of tests with positive (respectively negative) outcome within these ${{m}_{i}}$ tests. We then classify $i$ as follows:
\begin{equation} i =
    \left\{
    \begin{array}{ll}
    \text{non-defective} & \text{if} \; \frac{{{m}_{i}^+}}{{{m}_{i}}} \le \frac{{P(-,{q})}+{P(+,{q})}}{2}, \\
    \text{defective}     & \text{if} \; \frac{{{m}_{i}^+}}{{{m}_{i}}} > \frac{{P(-,{q})}+{P(+,{q})}}{2}.
    \end{array}
    \right.
\end{equation}

We then classify $i$ as follows:
