{\it Sensitivity parameter:}  Given a monotone test function $f(\cdot)$, its sensitivity parameter ${H(f)}$ is defined as 
\begin{align} {H(f)}:=\min_{0\leq {L}<{U} \leq d} \left(\frac{1}{\min\left\{{U}-{L} , \sqrt{{L}+1} , \sqrt{d-{U}+1}\right\}}\times \frac{{U}-{L}}{f({U})-f({L})} \right)^2. 
\end{align}

For any monotone test function $f(\cdot)$ and $d \geq 2$, we have\footnote{For $d=1$, we directly have ${H(f)}=\frac{1}{\left(f(d)-f(0)\right)^2}$ by definition.}
\begin{align} \frac{1}{\left(f(d)-f(0)\right)^2} \leq {H(f)}\leq \frac {16}{\left(f(d)-f(0)\right)^2}\left(\log\log d +2\right)^2 d.  
\end{align}

