Proposition \ref{prop:existence_final} is proved by unifying Propositions \ref{prop:existence} and  \ref{prop:existence_margin}. To begin with, consider any  ${L},{U} \in \{0,\dots, d\}$ such that ${L} < {U}$ and $f({L})<f({U})$.\footnote{If there is no such pair of $({L},{U})$, we have $f(x)$=constant for all $x$. In this case, the defective set $\mathcal{D}$ can never be recovered.}  
To simplify the notation, define
\begin{align} {\beta} := \min \left\{ {U}-{L} , \sqrt{{L}+1} , \sqrt{d-{U}+1} \right\}.
\end{align}
Consider the following four cases:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\roman*)]
\item $1 \le {L} < {U} \le d-1$; \item $0 = {L} < {U} \le d-1$; \item $1 \le {L} < {U} = d$; \item $0 = {L} < {U} = d$. \end{enumerate}


